R261	Addressee Arg1:T237 Arg2:T243	
T244	Quotation 49676 49703	"Ah, that is a fine thing,"
T245	Entity 49704 49706	he
T246	Cue 49707 49711	said
R262	Speaker Arg1:T245 Arg2:T244	
R263	Cueing Arg1:T246 Arg2:T244	
T247	Quotation 49748 49973	"That's a fine thing. You will come back from the high school such a gentleman that we shall all take off our hats to you. You will be wealthy and wise and so grand that your mamma will be delighted. Oh, that's a fine thing!"
R264	Speaker Arg1:T245 Arg2:T247	
R265	Cueing Arg1:T246 Arg2:T247	
R266	Addressee Arg1:T241 Arg2:T244	
R267	Addressee Arg1:T241 Arg2:T247	
T248	Quotation 50063 50361	"You must excuse me, Father Christopher, but I am thinking of writing to the bishop to tell him you are robbing the merchants of their living. I shall take a sheet of stamped paper and write that I suppose Father Christopher is short of pence, as he has taken up with trade and begun selling wool."
T249	Entity 49974 49976	He
R268	Speaker Arg1:T249 Arg2:T248	
T250	Cue 50017 50028	began again
R269	Cueing Arg1:T250 Arg2:T248	
T251	Quotation 50362 50413	"H'm, yes . . . it's a queer notion in my old age,"
T252	Cue 50414 50418	said
T253	Entity 50419 50437	Father Christopher
R270	Addressee Arg1:T253 Arg2:T248	
R271	Cueing Arg1:T252 Arg2:T251	
R272	Speaker Arg1:T253 Arg2:T251	
R273	Addressee Arg1:T249 Arg2:T251	
T254	Quotation 50455 50633	"I have turned from priest to merchant, brother. I ought to be at home now saying my prayers, instead of galloping about the country like a Pharaoh in his chariot. . . . Vanity!"
R274	Speaker Arg1:T253 Arg2:T254	
R275	Cueing Arg1:T252 Arg2:T254	
R276	Addressee Arg1:T249 Arg2:T254	
T255	Quotation 50634 50668	"But it will mean a lot of pence!"
R277	Speaker Arg1:T249 Arg2:T255	
R278	Addressee Arg1:T253 Arg2:T255	
T256	Quotation 50669 50783	"Oh, I dare say! More kicks than halfpence, and serve me right. The wool's not mine, but my son-in-law MikhailÕs!"
R279	Speaker Arg1:T253 Arg2:T256	
R280	Addressee Arg1:T249 Arg2:T256	
T257	Quotation 50784 50812	"Why doesn't he go himself?"
R281	Addressee Arg1:T253 Arg2:T257	
R282	Speaker Arg1:T249 Arg2:T257	
T258	Entity 51161 51163	he
T259	Entity 51172 51174	me
T260	Cue 51179 51183	said
T261	Quotation 51185 51268	'Daddy, you sell the wool for me; be kind and do it! I am no good at the business!'
R283	Cueing Arg1:T260 Arg2:T261	
R284	Addressee Arg1:T259 Arg2:T261	
R285	Speaker Arg1:T258 Arg2:T261	
T262	Quotation 50813 51625	"Why, because . . . His mother's milk is scarcely dry upon his lips. He can buy wool all right, but when it comes to selling, he has no sense; he is young yet. He has wasted all his money; he wanted to grow rich and cut a dash, but he tried here and there, and no one would give him his price. And so the lad went on like that for a year, and then he came to me and said, 'Daddy, you sell the wool for me; be kind and do it! I am no good at the business!' And that is true enough. As soon as there is anything wrong then it's 'Daddy,' but till then they could get on without their dad. When he was buying he did not consult me, but now when he is in difficulties it's Daddy's turn. And what does his dad know about it? If it were not for Ivan Ivanitch, his dad could do nothing. I have a lot of worry with them."
R286	Speaker Arg1:T253 Arg2:T262	
R287	Addressee Arg1:T249 Arg2:T262	
T263	Quotation 51626 51697	"Yes; one has a lot of worry with one's children, I can tell you that,"
T264	Cue 51698 51704	sighed
T265	Entity 51705 51722	Moisey Moisevitch
R288	Cueing Arg1:T264 Arg2:T263	
R289	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T263	
R290	Addressee Arg1:T253 Arg2:T263	
T266	Quotation 51724 52022	"I have six of my own. One needs schooling, another needs doctoring, and a third needs nursing, and when they grow up they are more trouble still. It is not only nowadays, it was the same in Holy Scripture. When Jacob had little children he wept, and when they grew up he wept still more bitterly."
R291	Cueing Arg1:T264 Arg2:T266	
R292	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T266	
T267	Entity 52040 52058	Father Christopher
R293	Addressee Arg1:T267 Arg2:T266	
T268	Quotation 52023 52039	"H'm, yes . . ."
R294	Speaker Arg1:T267 Arg2:T268	
T269	Cue 52059 52067	assented
R295	Cueing Arg1:T269 Arg2:T268	
R296	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T268	
T270	Quotation 52101 52941	"I have no cause myself to rail against the Lord. I have lived to the end of my days as any man might be thankful to live. . . . I have married my daughters to good men, my sons I have set up in life, and now I am free; I have done my work and can go where I like. I live in peace with my wife. I eat and drink and sleep and rejoice in my grandchildren, and say my prayers and want nothing more. I live on the fat of the land, and don't need to curry favour with anyone. I have never had any trouble from childhood, and now suppose the Tsar were to ask me, 'What do you need? What would you like?' why, I don't need anything. I have everything I want and everything to be thankful for. In the whole town there is no happier man than I am. My only trouble is I have so many sins, but there --only God is without sin. That's right, isn't it?"
R297	Speaker Arg1:T267 Arg2:T270	
R298	Cueing Arg1:T269 Arg2:T270	
R299	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T270	
T271	Quotation 52942 52959	"No doubt it is."
R300	Speaker Arg1:T265 Arg2:T271	
R301	Addressee Arg1:T267 Arg2:T271	
T272	Quotation 52960 53230	"I have no teeth, of course; my poor old back aches; there is one thing and another, . . . asthma and that sort of thing. . . . I ache. . . . The flesh is weak, but then think of my age! I am in the eighties! One can't go on for ever; one mustn't outstay one's welcome."
R302	Addressee Arg1:T265 Arg2:T272	
R303	Speaker Arg1:T267 Arg2:T272	
